248 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (ABSTRACT )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
249 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (Static Random Access Memory \(SRAM\) plays a vital role in modern digital systems due to its high speed, low latency, and compatibility with CMOS technology. Among various SRAM architectures, the 6-Transistor \(6T\) SRAM cell is widely used in cache memory and embedded systems because of its efficient design and fast operation. This project focuses on the analysis and performance evaluation of a 6T SRAM cell with emphasis on Static Noise Margin \(SNM\), propagation delay, and stacking effect.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
250 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (The stability of the SRAM cell is determined using SNM, which indicates the maximum noise voltage the cell can tolerate without losing stored data. SNM is evaluated using the butterfly curve obtained from the voltage transfer characteristics of cross-coupled inverters. A higher SNM value signifies better stability and reliability of the memory cell during read and hold operations.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
251 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (Propagation delay is another critical parameter that defines the speed of read and write operations. Delay analysis is carried out using transient simulations to measure the time required for data to be accessed or stored. The results help in understanding the trade-off between speed and stability in SRAM design.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (The stacking effect is studied as a technique to reduce leakage power in CMOS circuits. By connecting transistors in series, leakage current is significantly reduced due to increased effective resistance and reduced subthreshold conduction. This effect becomes particularly important in deep submicron technologies where leakage power is a major concern.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (The design and simulation of the 6T SRAM cell are implemented using standard CMOS technology in simulation tools such as Cadence. The results demonstrate that while stacking reduces leakage power, it may slightly impact delay, thereby highlighting the need for optimization.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 154 0 R
/E (Overall, this project provides a comprehensive understanding of SRAM cell design and the trade-offs between stability, speed, and power consumption, which are essential for developing efficient memory systems.r)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
