303 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (1.1 Overview Of SRAM)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
304 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (Memory plays an important role in digital systems because it stores and provides data required for processing operations. Among different memory types, Static Random Access Memory \(SRAM\) is widely used because of its high speed, low access time, and compatibility with CMOS technology. SRAM is mainly used in cache memory, register files, and embedded systems where fast data access is necessary.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
305 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (Unlike Dynamic Random Access Memory \(DRAM\), SRAM does not require periodic refreshing to retain data. This makes SRAM faster and more reliable during operation. However, SRAM occupies more chip area because each memory cell uses multiple transistors, and it also consumes higher static power due to leakage currents.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
306 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (The basic SRAM cell is the 6-Transistor \(6T\) SRAM cell, which consists of two cross-coupled inverters and two access transistors. The cross-coupled inverters store one bit of data, while the access transistors connect the cell to bit lines during read and write operations. The word line controls access to the cell.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
307 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (The performance of an SRAM cell is mainly evaluated using parameters such as Static Noise Margin \(SNM\), delay, and power consumption. SNM indicates the stability of the stored data and shows how much noise the cell can tolerate without changing its state. Delay represents the speed of read and write operations, while power consumption depends on switching activity and leakage current)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
308 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
309 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (To improve SRAM performance, techniques such as stacking effect and power gating are used. The stacking effect helps reduce leakage current by connecting transistors in series, while power gating reduces standby power using header and footer sleep transistors. In addition, sense amplifiers are used to detect small voltage differences during read operations and improve speed.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
310 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 157 0 R
/E (In this project, a 6T SRAM cell is designed and analyzed with focus on SNM, propagation delay, leakage reduction, and power efficiency. The objective is to study the trade-off between stability, speed, and power consumption in SRAM design using CMOS technology.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
