/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
325 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (The pull-up and pull-down transistors maintain the stored data through positive feedback between the cross-coupled inverters. This feedback helps the cell retain data as long as power is supplied.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
326 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (The operation of the 6T SRAM cell depends on transistor sizing because proper transistor ratio directly affects read stability, write ability, and hold performance. Usually, pull-down NMOS transistors are made stronger than access transistors to maintain stability during read operation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
327 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 158 0 R
/E (The 6T SRAM cell is widely preferred in VLSI memory design because of its simple architecture, fast access speed, and compatibility with CMOS fabrication technology. However, as technology scales down, maintaining cell stability and reducing leakage current become major design challenges.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
