378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (2.1 Existing Design of SRAM)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Different SRAM cell designs have been developed to improve memory performance in digital circuits. Among them, the 6T SRAM cell is the most commonly used design because of its simple structure and efficient operation. It consists of six MOS transistors, where four transistors form two cross-coupled inverters and two transistors act as access transistors.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (The 6T SRAM cell stores one bit of data and performs read, write, and hold operations through bit lines and word line control. It is widely used in cache memory and high-speed applications because of its fast access time and low latency.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (In addition to 6T SRAM, other designs such as 8T SRAM and 10T SRAM have also been proposed to improve stability and reduce leakage power. These designs add extra transistors to separate read and write paths, which improves reliability but increases area.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Among all designs, 6T SRAM remains widely preferred because it offers a good balance between area, speed, and power consumption in CMOS-based memory design.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Chapter 2: Literature Survey)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (2.1 Existing Design of SRAM)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (SRAM stability becomes a major concern as CMOS technology scales down to smaller dimensions. During read and write operations, the internal storage nodes of the SRAM cell may get disturbed, which can affect the stored data.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (One important challenge is reduced Static Noise Margin \(SNM\), which decreases the ability of the cell to tolerate noise. Lower supply voltage also affects the strength of transistors and reduces cell stability.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Leakage current is another challenge in SRAM design. As transistor size decreases, leakage power increases and may affect memory reliability.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 161 0 R
/E (Process variations such as changes in transistor dimensions and threshold voltage also influence SRAM performance. These variations can lead to unstable operation during hold, read, and write conditions)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
