395 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (In earlier works, SNM is commonly analyzed for three conditions: hold mode, read mode, and write mode. Among these, read SNM is usually lower because the stored data gets disturbed during read operation.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
396 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (The butterfly curve method is widely used for SNM analysis. It is obtained by plotting the voltage transfer characteristics of two cross-coupled inverters in the SRAM cell. The side length of the largest square inside the butterfly curve gives the SNM value.Previous studies show that transistor sizing, supply voltage, and leakage reduction techniques directly affect SNM performance of SRAM cells.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
397 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (2.4 Leakage Reduction Techniques)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
398 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Leakage power is a major challenge in SRAM design, especially as CMOS technology scales down. Leakage current causes power loss even when the memory cell is not actively switching, which affects overall efficiency.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
399 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Several techniques are used to reduce leakage power in SRAM cells. Common methods include stacking effect and power gating.Stacking effect reduces leakage current by connecting two or more transistors in series. When stacked transistors are in OFF condition, the leakage current decreases because the intermediate node voltage increases and reduces the current flow)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
400 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
401 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Power gating is another leakage reduction technique in which a sleep transistor is added between the SRAM cell and power supply or ground. During standby mode, the sleep transistor disconnects the circuit from supply, reducing leakage power.These techniques help improve power efficiency and are useful in designing low-power SRAM cells for modern VLSI applications.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
