724 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 180 0 R
/T (The performance of the 6T SRAM cell is evaluated based on key parameters such as SNM, delay, and power consumption obtained from Cadence simulations. The results indicate that the SRAM cell exhibits high stability during hold condition, while read and write operations show comparatively lower SNM due to operational disturbances. )
/E (The performance of the 6T SRAM cell is evaluated based on key parameters such as SNM, delay, and power consumption obtained from Cadence simulations. The results indicate that the SRAM cell exhibits high stability during hold condition, while read and write operations show comparatively lower SNM due to operational disturbances. )
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
725 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 180 0 R
/T (Delay analysis shows that the SRAM cell performs fast read and write operations, although a slight increase in delay is observed when power reduction techniques such as stacking and power gating are applied. This is due to the additional resistance introduced in the current path. Power analysis demonstrates that leakage power is significantly reduced using stacking effect and power gating techniques. The implementation of these techniques improves overall energy efficiency, especially during standby mode.)
/E (Delay analysis shows that the SRAM cell performs fast read and write operations, although a slight increase in delay is observed when power reduction techniques such as stacking and power gating are applied. This is due to the additional resistance introduced in the current path. Power analysis demonstrates that leakage power is significantly reduced using stacking effect and power gating techniques. The implementation of these techniques improves overall energy efficiency, especially during standby mode.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
726 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 180 0 R
/T (5.6 Comparison with conventional SRAM)
/E (5.6 Comparison with conventional SRAM)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 180 0 R
/T (The results show a comparison of SRAM performance under normal, header, footer, and combined power gating conditions. It is observed that the Hold SNM is highest in the Header+Footer configuration, indicating improved stability in standby mode. Read SNM decreases in power-gated designs due to added circuit complexity and voltage drops. Write SNM improves in optimized designs, showing better writability of the cell. Read delay is reduced in header and combined configurations, enhancing read speed. Write delay is also minimized in the Header+Footer case, indicating faster switching. Power consumption is reduced in certain configurations, especially in read operation with combined techniques. Overall, the results demonstrate a trade-off between stability, speed, and power, with the Header+Footer configuration providing the best overall performance.)
/E (The results show a comparison of SRAM performance under normal, header, footer, and combined power gating conditions. It is observed that the Hold SNM is highest in the Header+Footer configuration, indicating improved stability in standby mode. Read SNM decreases in power-gated designs due to added circuit complexity and voltage drops. Write SNM improves in optimized designs, showing better writability of the cell. Read delay is reduced in header and combined configurations, enhancing read speed. Write delay is also minimized in the Header+Footer case, indicating faster switching. Power consumption is reduced in certain configurations, especially in read operation with combined techniques. Overall, the results demonstrate a trade-off between stability, speed, and power, with the Header+Footer configuration providing the best overall performance.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
